From 77491f6364997c75f1bc63661cc3496c4bd9eafd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 04:01:39 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Tests in float: tolerances by solver and measured margins

- TwoLayerCube: the threshold of the L2 error in float depends on the
  linear solver, about ten times the largest error measured over the cases
  (4e-5 for CGSolver, HyprePCG and MUMPSSolver, 4e-4 for GMRESSolver and
  HypreGMRES, 1.2e-3 for HypreFGMRES) instead of 1e-3 for all: a shear
  modulus wrong by 2 % (L2 error of 3.1e-4 in the cases 1 and 2) is now
  detected with the first three solvers. The L2 error is printed.
- HeatTransferKernelsTest (1.1e-6 measured, tolerance 1e-5),
  BatchIntegrationTest (computations without assembly: 5.1e-4 in parallel,
  the reference being computed by MUMPS, tolerance 5e-3; 6.3e-3 in
  sequential, the reference being computed by GMRES and Jacobi without a
  sequential direct solver in single precision, tolerance 6e-2) and
  FixedPointSolverTest (4.4e-5): measured values updated in the comments,
  margins of about ten.
- HypreILUTest: on one process, the default level of fill is
  distinguished from zero in both types by the residual after three
  iterations with a null tolerance (2.3e-5 with ILU(0), 5.6e-7 with ILU(1)),
  float giving the same number of iterations to reach 1.2e-5. On two
  processes, both levels give close residuals in no stable order.
- PredictionTest uses the sequential solver of SequentialLinearSolver.hxx.
- MicromorphicDamage2DTest2 and MicromorphicDamage3DTest: initial residuals
  and residuals of the second alternate iteration measured in float given
  in the comments, justifying the relative tolerance 1e-2.
Double: 216/216 on the CPU, tolerances unchanged.
